    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. i'm in portland so it's been pretty rainy lately. i was looking at some organic stuff online too. is there a difference between using granular fertilizer vs liquid? also, i've noticed some weird little holes in the thatch, almost like something's been nibbling at the roots or the soil under the grass. is that normal for this time of year?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Granular fertilizer typically covers a larger area better for deep application, while liquid forms absorb faster but may not last as long; in Portland's heavy rain, granular usually requires more care to avoid washing nutrients straight into the soil rather than grass roots. As for the small holes you're noticing in the thatch, this is likely normal slugs or snails eating at the base of grass blades, especially in our wet weather, though they usually look like small dots in the morning rather than distinct deep holes. Since you mentioned your lawn is also thin, a core aeration followed by organic granular fertilizer might help aerate the soil and reduce thatch buildup effectively this season.",
